<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Cañari Confederation</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 1463 CE (approx.) · capital Guapondelig</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An alliance of chiefdoms in the southern highlands of Ecuador that fiercely resisted the Incas and later sided with the Spanish against them.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Cañari peoples occupied the southern highlands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Ecuador</a>, around the modern provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azuay-province">Azuay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canar-province">Cañar</a>, from the first millennium CE. Their chiefdoms formed a loose confederation, with Guapondelig, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuenca-ecuador">Cuenca</a>, as a leading centre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingapirca">Hatun Cañar</a>, now Ingapirca, as another.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Cañari were led by local chiefs who joined forces in war. They farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> and beans and were skilled metalworkers in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>. Their origin myth traced the people to a macaw and a flood, and they revered the moon. Burial sites such as Sigsig have yielded rich gold objects.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1463 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a> conquered the Cañari after hard resistance, and the Incas built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomebamba">Tomebamba</a> on the site of Guapondelig. Many Cañari were resettled as mitmaq. In the Inca civil war they backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a> and suffered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a>, so they allied with the Spanish in the 1530s.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 500 CE – 1463 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:69.65%;width:19.16%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 1463 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Guapondelig</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Cañari religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cañari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cañari, Kañari</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=982&amp;focus=canari-confederation" aria-label="Cañari Confederation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2508 2225 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cañari Confederation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2828 2481h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2828 2481h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ecuador. <a href="/atlas?year=982&amp;focus=canari-confederation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

